Deploy a web app to one Linux server and you have two mainstream options. Both are heavy.

**Docker** costs you 140 MB of RAM before your first container runs: `dockerd` at 89 MB plus `containerd` at 51 MB. On a 1 GB VPS, the kind of machine most side projects live on, that's 14% of the machine gone before your app starts. Every container adds another ~19 MB of supervisor overhead: a `containerd-shim` plus two `docker-proxy` processes per published port.

**Kubernetes on a single node** (k3s, MicroK8s) is the same problem, worse. More control-plane processes, more RAM spent on the platform than the workload.

The polished middle ground of **Dokku, Kamal, Coolify, and CapRover** gives you a real deploy loop and automatic TLS, but all of them run on Docker. So you inherit the daemon tax *and* something subtler: your machine now has two things that restart processes. systemd supervises the host; the container daemon supervises the containers. Two supervisors, one machine.

That's the question kuadrat starts from: **systemd already supervises your machine. Why add a second supervisor?**

## Quadlet solved half of this

Podman answered the runtime half years ago: no daemon, `podman run` is a direct fork. And since Podman 4.4, **Quadlet** extends that to the service layer. Write a `.container` file and systemd runs your container as a native unit:

```ini
# myapp.container: this IS a systemd unit
[Container]
Image=localhost/myapp:latest
PublishPort=3000:3000
```

No `dockerd`, no `containerd`, no proxy process per port. One 2.5 MB `conmon` per container, and systemd, which was already running, does the supervision.

But Quadlet is a **file format, not a deploy tool**. You still hand-write the unit. There's no "git repo to running service with TLS" path: no build, no rollout, no rollback, no healthcheck, no secrets, no single place to see what's running. Quadlet removed the daemon; it didn't add the deploy loop.

That's the gap **[kuadrat](https://github.com/rifkyputra/kuadrat)** fills.

## One command, six stages

```
kuadrat deploy myapp ~/apps/myapp
```

![Deploy loop state machine](/blog/kuadrat/deploy-loop.svg)

kuadrat takes a git repo and runs it through six stages: **Detect** the stack, **Build** the image with `podman build`, verify **Secrets** exist (fail early, so nothing serves half-configured), **Apply** a rendered Quadlet unit via `systemctl start`, **Route** it through Caddy for automatic Let's Encrypt TLS, and **Healthcheck** until the app answers.

If any stage fails, kuadrat rolls back in reverse from the failing stage. A failure before Apply touched nothing; the old version keeps serving. A failure after it re-applies the previous spec and unwinds the route. You get `RolledBack`. The only way to get `Failed` is if the rollback itself fails, and that one genuinely wants a human. If the host crashes mid-deploy, `kuadrat reconcile` (idempotent, safe on every boot) rolls back anything left in flight.

A daemon (`kuadrat serve`) adds a web UI with live deploy progress, logs, an SSE event stream, and a webhook. It binds loopback only; reach it over SSH tunnel or VPN.

## The design rules

The architecture (three Rust crates: core, CLI, daemon) reduces to four rules:

1. **systemd is the orchestrator.** kuadrat does not supervise, restart, or schedule. That's the whole point.
2. **The spec is the source of truth.** Unit files are derived artifacts kuadrat owns and may overwrite. Rollback means re-applying a previous spec, not diffing files.
3. **The core never touches the network.** Every host side effect goes through injectable seams, so the entire deploy loop is testable without root, Podman, or a live filesystem.
4. **Emit events, don't deliver them.** The daemon pushes typed events; the UI, the webhook, and an AI agent (via the shipped MCP surface) all consume the same stream. Notification delivery belongs to subscribers.

## The numbers

The RAM claim is testable, so I tested it: the same app, byte-identical `app.py` and base image, deployed once via kuadrat and once via Docker Compose. Supervisor processes only, not the app. Ubuntu 24.04, Podman 4.9.3 vs Docker 29.1.3:

![Benchmark: Docker vs kuadrat RAM](/blog/kuadrat/benchmark.svg)

| | Docker | kuadrat |
|---|---|---|
| **Fixed** (before any container) | `dockerd` 88.8 MB + `containerd` 51.5 MB = **140.3 MB** | **0**: systemd is already running |
| **Marginal** (per container) | shim 10.4 MB + 2× proxy 8.9 MB = **19.3 MB** | `conmon` **2.5 MB** |
| **10 containers** | **334 MB** | **25 MB** |

**7.8× less RAM per container**, and Docker pays its 140 MB before the first one starts.

Two honest caveats. The measurement is **RSS, not PSS**, which double-counts shared pages and inflates the multi-process (Docker) side. The direction is not in doubt, but the ratio would shrink under PSS. And the host was already running ~16 other Docker containers, so `dockerd`'s working set is fatter than a clean host's; the marginal figures are unaffected. Throughput showed **no difference**. One run suggested kuadrat was faster, it didn't reproduce, and that's the honest result: both runtimes start the same process under the same kernel. Full method in [the benchmark README](https://github.com/rifkyputra/kuadrat/blob/main/examples/hello-py-docker/README.md).

## Compared to what else

| | Deploy loop | Gateway / TLS | Agent surface | Runs on Quadlet |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **kuadrat** | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |
| [Dokku](https://dokku.com) | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ Docker |
| [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org) | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ Docker |
| [Coolify](https://coolify.io) | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ Docker |
| [CapRover](https://caprover.com) | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ Docker |
| [quadit](https://crates.io/crates/quadit) | ✅ GitOps | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ |
| [quadletman](https://github.com/mikkovihonen/quadletman) | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ |
| [podlet](https://github.com/containers/podlet) | ❌ one-shot | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ |
| Cockpit | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ |

The Docker PaaS family does deploy loop + TLS well (these are mature projects) but pays Docker's fixed and per-container costs. The Quadlet tools each handle one piece; none wraps the full loop. **The unoccupied combination is deploy loop + gateway/TLS + agent-operable surface, on Quadlet.** That's kuadrat's space.

## Status, and try it

kuadrat is **pre-alpha**. The deploy loop, rollback, secrets, crash reconciliation, and the daemon with web UI and webhook are merged and working. Not in v1: multi-host orchestration, blue/green deploys, push-to-deploy, metrics, autonomous agent action.

```bash
git clone https://github.com/rifkyputra/kuadrat.git && cd kuadrat
cargo build --release
sudo kuadrat deploy hello examples/hello-py
```

Needs Podman 4.4+, systemd on cgroups v2, and `git`; Caddy only if you want the Route stage. Architecture seams, the full benchmark method, and design ADRs are in the [README and docs](https://github.com/rifkyputra/kuadrat).
